## Why This Is Asked

Tests a new DP angle on strings with deletions, requiring careful handling of multiple transitional options (match, delete ends, delete both) and reconstruction under a budget. It extends longest palindromic subsequence with a deletions constraint, common in intermediate interviews.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP state dp[i][j][t]
- Transitions: match, delete left, delete right, delete both
- Reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time/space: O(n^2 k)
- Edge cases: k may exceed n, i==j base

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalLenWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][i][0]=1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++){
        if (s[i]===s[j] && t>=0){
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], (i+1<=j-1 ? dp[i+1][j-1][t] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (t>0){
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j][t-1], dp[i][j-1][t-1]);
        }
        if (t>1){
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i+1][j-1][t-2]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) ans = Math.max(ans, dp[0][n-1][t]);
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify to return the actual palindrome string? 
- What optimizations reduce space to O(n^2) or O(n) with careful layering?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming skills for selecting exactly k disjoint subarrays with a per-subarray penalty—a common variant in coding interviews that extends standard maximum subarray problems.

## Key Concepts

- **Non-overlapping constraint**: Subarrays must not intersect, handled by the state machine.
- **Dual-state DP**: Maintaining both global (dp) and local (end) states to track ongoing vs. completed selections.
- **Reconstruction**: Tracing back decisions to recover actual subarray endpoints.
- **Optimization**: Balancing time complexity (O(nk)) against space (full vs. rolling arrays).

## Code Example

```javascript
const solve = (A, k, c) => {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const end = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Not taking a subarray ending at i
      if (dp[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'skip';
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = end[i][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'end';
      }

      // Taking/starting subarray at i
      if (t > 0) {
        const startNew = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c;
        const extend = end[i-1][t] + A[i-1] - c;
        end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction logic would go here
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: [] };
};
```

## Complexity

- **Time:** O(nk)
- **Space:** O(nk) for full DP, O(k) for optimized version

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an optimized DP on a circular constraint and track the number of optimal solutions.

## Key Concepts
- DP on linear sequences with spacing (dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-d] + v[i]))
- Reducing circularity to two linear cases (include first vs exclude first)
- Counting distinct optimal subsets with tie handling (sum counts when equal)

## Code Example
```javascript
function linearDP(arr, d){
  const n = arr.length; const dp = Array(n+1).fill(0); const ways = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    const take = arr[i-1] + (i-d>=0 ? dp[i-d] : 0);
    const skip = dp[i-1];
    if(take>skip){ dp[i]=take; ways[i]=(i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1); }
    else if(take<skip){ dp[i]=skip; ways[i]=ways[i-1]; }
    else { dp[i]=skip; ways[i] = ((i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1) + ways[i-1]); }
  }
  return [dp[n], ways[n]];
}

function maxCircular(v, d){
  const [bestA, cntA] = linearDP(v.slice(1), d); // exclude first
  const n = v.length;
  const seg = (n > 2*d-1) ? v.slice(d, n-d+1) : [];
  const [bestBseg, cntBseg] = seg.length ? linearDP(seg, d) : [0,1];
  const bestB = v[0] + bestBseg;
  if(bestA > bestB) return [bestA, cntA];
  if(bestB > bestA) return [bestB, cntBseg];
  return [bestA, cntA + cntBseg];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow a variable distance per pair or weighted distance penalties?
- How would you test correctness and performance on worst-case inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to model partitioning with a non-linear block value, a common DP pattern in beginner interviews. Requires prefix sums for O(1) block sums, a clear state definition over prefix length and blocks, and backtracking to output endpoints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on partitions with exact k blocks
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- State: dp[i][t], parent pointers for reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxRevenue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = S[i]*i;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=t;i<=n;i++) {
      for (let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + (S[i]-S[p])*(i-p);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i=n, t=k; const ends=[];
  while (t>0) { const p = par[i][t]; ends.push([p+1, i]); i = p; t--; }
  ends.reverse();
  return {value: dp[n][k], windows: ends};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a trick for monotone optima?
- How would you adapt if windows could overlap or if k is variable?

## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates the ability to design a dynamic programming solution for selecting exactly k vertex-disjoint directed paths in a DAG while maximizing total node weights. It is a common pattern in interviews at Netflix, Uber, and Meta.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological ordering** ensures DP processes vertices in valid dependency order
- **DP over path count dimension** (t from 1 to k) tracks the maximum weight for t paths ending at each vertex
- **Vertex-disjoint constraint** is naturally enforced through the state definition, since each path is built incrementally
- **Parent pointer array** enables reconstruction of the selected paths after DP computation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code for k vertex-disjoint path selection

function selectKPaths(N, M, edges, weights, k) {
  const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  // Get topological order
  const topo = topologicalSort(N, edges);

  // Initialize sources
  for (const s of sources) {
    dp[s][1] = weights[s];
  }

  // Process in topological order
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 1; t < k; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
          const newWeight = dp[u][t] + weights[v];
          if (newWeight > dp[v][t + 1]) {
            dp[v][t + 1] = newWeight;
            parent[v][t + 1] = u;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct paths using parent pointers
  return reconstructPaths(dp, parent, k);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests substring DP and reconstruction in a practical string-cleanup task, common in data processing pipelines at scale.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings: dp[i][j] captures minimal deletions for s[i..j]
- Reconstruction: recover exact deletions and resulting palindrome
- Complexity: O(n^2) time and O(n^2) space; can be optimized with path tracking

## Code Example
```javascript
function minDeletionsPalindrome(s) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len - 1 < n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      if (s[i] === s[j]) dp[i][j] = dp[i+1][j-1];
      else dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1]) + 1;
    }
  }
  return {deletions: dp[0][n-1], dp};
}
```

